\label{throwawayFrattini}
Let $G$ be a group.
If $X$ is a non-empty subset of $G$ with $G = \langle \Phi(G), X\rangle$, then $G= \langle X\rangle$.

\label{normal,containedinafrattini}
Let $G$ be a group.
If $K$ is a normal subgroup of $G$ with $K \leqslant \Phi(H)$ for some subgroup $H$ of $G$, then $K \leqslant \Phi(G)$.

\label{frattinisubgroupofdirectproduct}
Let $G$ be a group and assume that $G = J \times R$.
Then $\Phi(G) = \Phi(J) \times \Phi(R)$.
